what this proves : two frames of the SAME sandbox instance, the second taken
after the first drove a real lobby session, agree on every face's HTTP status
and error set. A single-frame claim cannot say that.

what it does NOT prove : that the pages' content is correct or current, that a
buyer can pay (the checkout face is the mock form, chargeable=false, no
merchant id / server / domain / filing+ICP / appid+secret), or that the two
other published faces (panel, member) are up - start_all starts four processes
and those two launch separately, so the cohort measured here is seven of nine.

defect caught by RUNNING this window : the first --prove run read rc=2 with a
perfect proof block, because the payload verdict still measured len(rows)
against len(FACES) while --prove holds two passes (14 rows). Repaired to count
DISTINCT faces, then the live arm was re-run and read rc=0.

suite red controls (stdlib, qa/live-proof-R2935.log) : status drift refuses with
both values, restoring the value restores agreement, an extra error refuses
while a reordered error set does not, a face present in one pass only refuses as
missing in both directions, and a face drifting in two ways is counted once so
'agreements + disagreements == faces' stays an identity.
